 exited 1 in 2962ms:
   1: # Workflow Builder â€” User Guide
   2: 
   3: The workflow builder is the default surface of ScriptToScene Studio: a node
   4: canvas where you assemble, validate, and run video-production pipelines. Every
   5: node wraps a real pipeline step (TTS, alignment, segmentation, scene AI, asset
   6: generation, captions, music, assembly, export) and writes the same artifacts as
   7: the legacy step pages.
   8: 
   9: Companion reference: [workflow-nodes.md](workflow-nodes.md) â€” every node's
  10: ports, configuration fields, defaults, and capabilities, generated directly
  11: from the backend registry (`python -m studio.workflows.docs`).
  12: 
  13: ## Before you begin
  14: 
  15: Complete the repository [prerequisites and installation](../README.md#prerequisites)
  16: once before following this guide. For day-to-day development, run
  17: `start-dev.bat`; for the production build, run `start-prod.bat`. The Full Video
  18: template invokes the configured scene, storyboard, and animator providers, so
  19: configure their URLs or credentials in **Settings** (and `.env` where
  20: applicable) before the first real run.
  21: 
  22: ---
  23: 
  24: ## 1. Quick start â€” run the Full Video template
  25: 
  26: 1. Start the app (`start-dev.bat`) and open it in the browser. You land on the
  27:    workflow builder (`#/workflow`) automatically.
  28: 2. In the toolbar, open the **Templateâ€¦** dropdown and pick **Full Video**.
  29:    A fully connected pipeline appears: Manual Trigger â†’ Project Setup +
  30:    Script Input â†’ Text to Speech â†’ Force Alignment â†’ Segmenter â†’
  31:    Scene Blueprint â†’ Storyboard â†’ Animator â†’ (with Captions and Background
  32:    Music) â†’ Assemble Project â†’ Timeline Project â†’ Video Export â†’
  33:    Workflow Output.
  34: 3. Click the **Script Input** node. In the right inspector, paste your
  35:    narration script into the **Script** field (this is the only required field
  36:    the template leaves blank).
  37: 4. Optionally click **Project Setup** and choose tone, visual style, aspect
  38:    ratio, and branding (enable **Show logo on video** to upload a logo that is
  39:    watermarked onto every export).
  40: 5. Click **Validate** in the toolbar. You should see "Workflow is valid".
  41: 6. Click **Save**, give the workflow a name. Saving enables run history.
  42: 7. Leave the run-mode dropdown on **Full workflow** and click **â–¶ Run**.
  43:    Nodes light up as they execute (blue = running, green = succeeded,
  44:    red = failed); edges animate while data flows.
  45: 8. When the run finishes, the bottom panel shows every node's status and
  46:    duration. Click **Open in Timeline Editor** to fine-tune the assembled
  47:    project, or click the **Video Export** node in the timeline pane to see the
  48:    rendered file's path under `output/exports/<project_id>/`.
  49: 
  50: Other built-in templates: **Narration Only** (script â†’ audio),
  51: **Storyboard Only** (script â†’ reference images), and **Re-export Existing
  52: Project** (existing timeline project â†’ new export).
  53: 
  54: ---
  55: 
  56: ## 2. The workspace
  57: 
  58: | Region | Purpose |
  59: |---|---|
  60: | Left panel | Node library: search box ("Search nodesâ€¦"), a **Recently used** section, and all nodes grouped by colored category. Drag a node onto the canvas to add it. |
  61: | Canvas | The graph itself: nodes, typed connections, sticky notes, minimap, zoom controls, 20 px snap grid. |
  62: | Right panel | Inspector for the selected node: name, enable/disable, configuration form, error policy, and the **Workflow variables** editor. |
  63: | Bottom panel | "Runs & diagnostics": run history, node timeline, and per-node deep inspection. |
  64: | Toolbar | File actions (New / Openâ€¦ / Templateâ€¦ / Save / More), Undo/Redo, Validate, run controls, Tidy / Fit / Auto-stubs, and the legacy **Pipeline** link. |
  65: 
  66: ### Canvas basics
  67: 
  68: - **Connect nodes** by dragging from an output handle to an input handle.
  69:   Connections are typed: a port only accepts a connection of the **same**
  70:   type (see the port tables in [workflow-nodes.md](workflow-nodes.md)).
  71:   Incompatible targets are rejected with a plain-language toast; compatible
  72:   handles highlight while you drag.
  73: - **Drop a connection on empty canvas** to open the "Insert compatible node"
  74:   palette â€” pick a node and it is inserted and connected in one step.
  75: - **Right-click** a node, edge, note, or the empty canvas for context menus:
  76:   Copy, Duplicate, Enable/Disable, Replace withâ€¦, run actions, Attach sample
  77:   inputs / Attach result viewer, Delete; edges offer Disconnect; the canvas
  78:   offers Paste here, Add note, and Auto arrange.
  79: - **Tidy** auto-arranges the graph left-to-right; **Fit** frames everything.
  80: - **Sticky notes** annotate the canvas and never execute.
  81: 
  82: ### Keyboard shortcuts
  83: 
  84: | Shortcut | Action |
  85: |---|---|
  86: | Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Shift+Z | Undo / Redo (every canvas operation is undoable) |
  87: | Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V | Copy / paste selected nodes (works across workflows) |
  88: | Ctrl+D | Duplicate selection |
  89: | Delete | Remove selected nodes / edges / notes |
  90: | Ctrl+Click | Add or remove a node from the multi-selection |
  91: 
  92: ---
  93: 
  94: ## 3. Configuring nodes
  95: 
  96: Select a node to edit it in the inspector. Forms are generated from each
  97: node's schema â€” text, numbers, toggles, dropdowns, JSON editors, and the
  98: logo's media-asset picker (upload new or pick an existing image; raw file
  99: paths are never accepted).
 100: 
 101: - **Conditional fields** appear only when relevant (e.g. the logo position
 102:   controls only show once **Show logo on video** is enabled; Grok-specific
 103:   animator options only show for that provider).
 104: - **Live option lists** (voices, tones, style templates, providers, export
 105:   profiles, caption presets) are fetched from the backend, so they always
 106:   match what the server accepts.
 107: - **Project settings inheritance:** nodes with a `settings` input inherit
 108:   tone/style/aspect-ratio defaults from **Project Setup**; a value set
 109:   directly on the node always wins over the inherited one.
 110: - **Error policy:** where a node supports it, choose what a failure does â€”
 111:   stop the run, retry with backoff, continue through the explicit `error`
 112:   control output, or skip when optional.
 113: - **Expressions:** any config field can be mapped from an upstream output via
 114:   the "Map from upstream outputâ€¦" picker, which writes an expression such as
 115:   `{{ nodes.<id>.outputs.<port> }}`. `{{ workflow.project_id }}` and
 116:   `{{ variables.<name> }}` are also available; variables are edited as JSON in
 117:   the inspector's **Workflow variables** section. Expressions are whole-value
 118:   only (no interpolation or code) and may reference only upstream nodes.
 119: 
 120: ### Validation
 121: 
 122: Invalid nodes show a badge with the number of issues (hover to read them).
 123: The **Validate** toolbar button asks the server for an authoritative check and
 124: reports problems and warnings. The same validation runs on save, and a save is
 125: blocked â€” with the reason shown in the toolbar â€” while any JSON field holds
 126: unparseable text.
 127: 
 128: ---
 129: 
 130: ## 4. Sample-data stubs (test nodes in isolation)
 131: 
 132: You do not need a full pipeline to try a node:
 133: 
 134: - With **Auto-stubs** enabled in the toolbar (default), dropping a node with
 135:   unconnected required inputs automatically spawns one dashed **Sample Input**
 136:   stub per required input, pre-filled with a realistic fixture payload, plus a
 137:   **Result Viewer** on the node's main output.
 138: - Edit a stub's payload in the inspector; payloads are validated against the
 139:   port type.
 140: - Connecting a real edge to a stubbed input removes the stub (Ctrl+Z brings
 141:   it back).
 142: - Add stubs manually from the **Testing** library category or via the node
 143:   context menu ("Attach sample inputs" / "Attach result viewer").
 144: - Run the node with the **Node in isolation** run mode: Sample Inputs return
 145:   their payloads instantly and every downstream result is marked **sample** in
 146:   the timeline and run record, so test data can never be mistaken for real
 147:   output.
 148: - **Pinning:** enable **Pin edited result** on a Result Viewer to freeze its
 149:   payload. The pinned value feeds downstream nodes (shown as a "pinned" badge)
 150:   until unpinned â€” useful for iterating on late pipeline stages without
 151:   re-running expensive upstream nodes.
 152: 
 153: ---
 154: 
 155: ## 5. Running workflows
 156: 
 157: Pick a mode in the run dropdown (or use the node context menu), then **â–¶ Run**:
 158: 
 159: | Mode | Runs |
 160: |---|---|
 161: | Full workflow | Every enabled node. |
 162: | Node + dependencies | The selected node and everything it depends on. |
 163: | Node in isolation | Only the selected node, fed by its Sample Input stubs. |
 164: | Selected + dependencies | All selected nodes plus their dependencies. |
 165: | From node downstream | The selected node and everything after it. |
 166: | Retry failed node | Re-runs one failed node, keeping all other results. |
 167: | Retry failed + downstream | The failed node plus its descendants. |
 168: 
 169: While running:
 170: 
 171: - Node colors show status: gray = idle/queued, blue = running,
 172:   purple = waiting, green = succeeded, red = failed, amber = cancelled,
 173:   slate = skipped, orange = stale.
 174: - **â–  Stop** cancels cooperatively; a cancelled run never reports success.
 175: - Progress streams live; dropped connections reconnect automatically and
 176:   replay the events they missed, and finished runs are always available in
 177:   run history.
 178: 
 179: ### Caching and staleness
 180: 
 181: Successful node results are cached by a fingerprint of the node's type,
 182: configuration, and upstream artifacts. Re-running an unchanged workflow
 183: re-executes nothing; changing one node marks it and its descendants **stale**
 184: (orange) and re-executes exactly that subgraph. Renaming or moving nodes never
 185: invalidates the cache. Each run records the cache hit/miss decision and reason
 186: per node â€” visible in the bottom panel.
 187: 
 188: ### Diagnosing a run
 189: 
 190: The bottom panel ("Runs & diagnostics") has three panes:
 191: 
 192: 1. **Run history** â€” every persisted run for the saved workflow, newest first,
 193:    with status, mode, and duration. Click one to inspect it.
 194: 2. **Node timeline** â€” per-node status, duration bars, attempt counts, and
 195:    **sample** markers.
 196: 3. **Detail** â€” click a finished node to see its cache decision, resolved
 197:    inputs, outputs, artifact references, structured errors with recovery
 198:    suggestions, per-attempt errors, and logs. Failed nodes offer **Retry
 199:    failed** and **Retry + downstream** buttons that preserve all successful
 200:    work.
 201: 
 202: When a run produces an editor project, **Open in Timeline Editor** jumps to it
 203: with the project preloaded.
 204: 
 205: ---
 206: 
 207: ## 6. Saving, drafts, and portability
 208: 
 209: - **Save / Save asâ€¦ / Duplicate workflow** live in the toolbar; the yellow
 210:   **Unsaved** badge shows dirty state.
 211: - **Draft autosave:** edits are snapshotted to the browser about once a second.
 212:   If the tab closes or crashes mid-edit, reopening the page offers to recover
 213:   the draft. Explicit save clears the dirty state; leaving with unsaved
 214:   changes asks for confirmation (the draft is kept either way).
 215: - **Import JSONâ€¦ / Export JSON** exchange workflow files with other machines;
 216:   imports are validated (size, schema, node types) before anything is saved.
 217: - Deleting a workflow moves it to `output/TRASH/workflows/` rather than
 218:   erasing it.
 219: 
 220: ---
 221: 
 222: ## 7. Legacy pages
 223: 
 224: The step-by-step dashboard remains available: toolbar â†’ **Pipeline**, or the
 225: sidebar's "Legacy Pipeline Dashboard". Each legacy page links back to the
 226: workflow builder. Both surfaces operate on the same projects and artifacts
 227: under `output/`, so you can mix them freely â€” e.g. run the workflow, then
 228: polish in the Timeline Editor, then re-export from either surface.
 229: 
 230: ---
 231: 
 232: ## 8. Where things live
 233: 
 234: | What | Where |
 235: |---|---|
 236: | Saved workflows | `output/workflows/<wf_id>.json` |
 237: | Run records | `output/workflows/executions/` |
 238: | Project artifacts | `output/<step>/<project_id>/` |
 239: | Final exports | `output/exports/<project_id>/` |
 240: | Branding / logos | `output/branding/` |
 241: | Node reference | [docs/workflow-nodes.md](workflow-nodes.md) (generated â€” do not edit) |
