## Findings

- **[P1] Fresh-install production quick start never builds the frontend** — [README.md](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/README.md:502) instructs users to run `npm install`, then `start-prod.bat`. However, `static/dist/` is ignored and absent from a clean clone, while [start-prod.bat](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/start-prod.bat:68) launches Flask without running `npm run build`. Flask consequently has no `static/dist/index.html` to serve. Add `npm run build` to the onboarding steps or make the production launcher build/synchronize the frontend.

- **[P2] The documented development quick start opens the wrong interface** — [workflow-guide.md](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/docs/workflow-guide.md:26) says `start-dev.bat` lands on `#/workflow`, after which the newcomer should use the Template dropdown. In reality, [start-dev.bat](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/start-dev.bat:188) explicitly opens `#/pipeline`, where that dropdown does not exist. Point the launcher to `#/workflow`, or document the navigation required to reach Workflow Builder.

The node-reference generator itself passed adversarial inspection: the committed output matches fresh generation, and registry/template coverage is guarded.

Verification completed:

- Documentation suite: `9 passed, 23 subtests passed`
- Focused hardening suite: `56 passed, 23 subtests passed`
- `python -m studio.workflows.docs --check`: passed
- Worktree remained unchanged.
